<!doctype html>
<html lang="{{.language}}">
<head>
<meta charset="utf-8">
<meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1">
<title>{{.title}}</title>
<meta name="description" content="{{.description}}">
<link rel="canonical" href="{{.url}}">
<meta property="og:type" content="website">
<meta property="og:url" content="{{.url}}">
<meta property="og:title" content="{{.title}}">
<meta property="og:description" content="{{.description}}">
<meta property="og:image" content="{{.socialImage.src}}">
<meta property="og:image:alt" content="{{.socialImage.alt}}">
<meta name="twitter:card" content="summary_large_image">
<link rel="alternate" type="text/markdown" title="Instructions for agents" href="assets/agents/SKILL.md">
<link rel="preload" href="assets/fonts/martian-mono.woff2" as="font" type="font/woff2" crossorigin>
<link rel="preload" href="assets/fonts/atkinson-hyperlegible-next.woff2" as="font" type="font/woff2" crossorigin>
{{style "assets/styles/main.css"}}
</head>
<body>
<main>

<article class="tape tape-top" aria-labelledby="headline">
  <header class="till">
    <p class="store" data-design-reason="the till header of the receipt concept: store line and register above the headline">LFM.CASH</p>
    <p class="row small"><span>Register 01</span><span class="today">Open today</span></p>
  </header>
  <p class="rule" aria-hidden="true"></p>
  <div class="hero">
    <div class="pitch">
      <h1 id="headline">Nobody screenshots a dead product.</h1>
      <p class="row total"><span>Sales today</span><span class="red">$0.00</span></p>
      <p class="lede">So sell before you build. lfm gives your coding agent the process: your buyers' real posts first, then one checked page with your payment link. Build the product once someone pays.</p>
    </div>

    <section class="coupon" aria-labelledby="coupon-h">
      <h2 id="coupon-h">Paste this into your agent</h2>
      <form id="printer" class="printer" action="#coupon-h">
        <label for="what">What do you sell? <span class="optional">(optional)</span></label>
        <input id="what" type="text" placeholder="{{.data.prompt.example}}" maxlength="60" autocomplete="off" spellcheck="false">
        <div class="slip" id="slip">
          <p class="row small slip-head"><span>Order</span><span id="slip-what">{{.data.prompt.fallback}}</span></p>
          <p class="prompt" id="prompt" aria-live="polite">Make a landing page for <span id="prompt-what">{{.data.prompt.fallback}}</span> with&nbsp;lfm: <span class="url">{{.data.prompt.skill}}</span></p>
          <p class="row small slip-foot"><span>Sales today</span><span>$0.00</span></p>
          <p class="stamp" aria-hidden="true">Copied</p>
        </div>
        <button type="submit" class="copy" id="copy">Copy my prompt</button>
      </form>
      <p class="trust">Free, MIT license, no account. The link is a plain skill file: your agent installs one checksummed binary from the GitHub releases into ~/.local/bin, then runs lfm in a new project folder. <a href="assets/agents/SKILL.md">Read it first</a>.</p>
    </section>
  </div>
  <p class="tear" aria-hidden="true"></p>
</article>

<section class="nodm" aria-labelledby="nodm-h">
  <h2 id="nodm-h"><s>Comment PROMPT and I'll DM you.</s></h2>
  <p>No DM. The line above is the whole prompt. Your agent reads the rest from the link in it.</p>
</section>

<article class="tape" aria-labelledby="usual-h">
  <p class="tear tear-in" aria-hidden="true"></p>
  <h2 id="usual-h">The usual receipt</h2>
  <ul class="items">
    <li class="row"><span>Auth, billing, dashboard</span><span>done</span></li>
    <li class="row"><span>The feature nobody asked for</span><span>done</span></li>
    <li class="row"><span>Landing page</span><span>night before</span></li>
    <li class="row"><span>Launch post</span><span>sent</span></li>
    <li class="row"><span>Replies</span><span>none</span></li>
  </ul>
  <p class="row total"><span>Sales</span><span class="red">$0.00</span></p>
  <p>Shipping got fast. The silence after launch did not: zero users, zero replies, $0. Building feels like progress because a build answers in seconds. A launch post answers in days, if at all, so the next feature always wins.</p>
  <p class="tear" aria-hidden="true"></p>
</article>

<article class="tape" aria-labelledby="first-h">
  <p class="tear tear-in" aria-hidden="true"></p>
  <h2 id="first-h">Sell it first. Build it when someone pays.</h2>
  <ul class="items">
    <li class="row"><span>Research</span><span>{{.data.minimums.voices}}+ real posts</span></li>
    <li class="row"><span>Concept</span><span>every choice cited</span></li>
    <li class="row"><span>Page</span><span>{{.data.rules}} checks</span></li>
    <li class="row"><span>Payment link</span><span>yours</span></li>
    <li class="row"><span>Selling</span><span>2 or 3 days</span></li>
  </ul>
  <p class="row total"><span>Total</span><span class="red">a sale or a no</span></p>
  <p>Demand first, product second. Your agent collects what buyers actually write, at least {{.data.minimums.voices}} posts from {{.data.minimums.platforms}} platforms and {{.data.minimums.peers}} competing pages, decides the page from that evidence and builds it. You put your offer and a payment link on it and start with the sale: post where the research found your buyers, deliver the first orders by hand. Nobody pays, and you lost days instead of months. Someone pays, and you know what to build.</p>
  <p>One founder made $0 for 90 days because the buy buttons still opened a "payments coming soon" box. Remove the waitlist copy the day checkout works; lfm flags a checkout link that is missing or hidden.</p>
  <p class="tear" aria-hidden="true"></p>
</article>

<article class="tape" aria-labelledby="after-h">
  <p class="tear tear-in" aria-hidden="true"></p>
  <h2 id="after-h">After you paste</h2>
  <ul class="items">
    <li class="row"><span>Agent installs lfm</span><span>1 binary</span></li>
    <li class="row"><span>Agent reads buyers</span><span>{{.data.minimums.voices}}+ posts</span></li>
    <li class="row"><span>Agent writes concept.md</span><span>cited</span></li>
    <li class="row"><span>Agent builds the page</span><span>0 warnings</span></li>
    <li class="row"><span>You add a payment link</span><span>Stripe</span></li>
    <li class="row"><span>You upload output/</span><span>any host</span></li>
  </ul>
  <p class="row total"><span>Your part</span><span class="red">2 steps</span></p>
  <p>The agent asks you only what you decide: the product, the price, how it is delivered and your legal pages. The research needs web access; the agent uses search, a browser or connectors when it has them and tells you what it could not reach. For this page that was {{.data.receipt.voices}} posts and {{.data.receipt.peers}} competing pages in {{.data.receipt.researchTime}}, with four readers in parallel.</p>
  <p class="tear" aria-hidden="true"></p>
</article>

<article class="tape" aria-labelledby="void-h">
  <p class="tear tear-in" aria-hidden="true"></p>
  <h2 id="void-h">Rung up: the page your agent writes by default</h2>
  <p>We asked an agent with no research and no lfm for a landing page for a made-up Slack summarizer. One sentence in, one file out. Then lfm compiled it.</p>
  <figure class="voided">
    <div class="shot">
      <img src="assets/images/default-agent-page-480.webp" srcset="assets/images/default-agent-page-480.webp 480w, assets/images/default-agent-page-816.webp 816w" sizes="17rem" alt="The agent's page at phone width: a beige ground, the serif headline 147 replies. Three decisions. Read those. with the middle line in orange italics, a pill reading Works inside Slack above it and an orange trial button." width="480" height="1039">
      <p class="void-stamp" aria-hidden="true">Void</p>
    </div>
    <figcaption>The agent's page at phone width, as it wrote it.</figcaption>
  </figure>
  <ul class="items findings">
    <li><p class="row"><span>Main button text</span><span class="red">{{.data.voided.buttonContrast}}</span></p><p class="rule-id">release.layout, and {{.data.voided.lowContrastMore}} more texts under 4.5:1</p></li>
    <li><p class="row"><span>Label over every heading</span><span>×6</span></p><p class="rule-id">design.eyebrows</p></li>
    <li><p class="row"><span>Pill badge over the headline</span><span>×1</span></p><p class="rule-id">design.hero</p></li>
    <li><p class="row"><span>Italic accent, pills, blur</span><span>{{.data.voided.defaults}} tells</span></p><p class="rule-id">design.defaults: generator defaults combined</p></li>
    <li><p class="row"><span>No canonical URL</span><span class="red">error</span></p><p class="rule-id">seo.metadata: search engines cannot tell which copy is the page</p></li>
    <li><p class="row"><span>No share preview</span><span>×1</span></p><p class="rule-id">seo.social</p></li>
    <li><p class="row"><span>Two links, one label</span><span>×1</span></p><p class="rule-id">a11y.link-purpose</p></li>
  </ul>
  <p class="row total"><span>output/</span><span class="red">not written</span></p>
  <p>{{.data.voided.errors}} error and {{.data.voided.warnings}} warnings from the first compile, plus the contrast finding of the browser audit. Each is printed with its fix, so your agent repairs them before anyone sees the page. Read <a href="{{.urls.proofReport}}">the full report</a> and <a href="{{.urls.proofSource}}">the agent's file</a>.</p>
  <p class="tear" aria-hidden="true"></p>
</article>

<article class="tape" aria-labelledby="look-h">
  <p class="tear tear-in" aria-hidden="true"></p>
  <h2 id="look-h">Won't it just make the lfm look?</h2>
  <ul class="items">
    <li class="row"><span>House style</span><span>none</span></li>
    <li class="row"><span>This page</span><span>a till receipt</span></li>
    <li class="row"><span>Why a receipt</span><span>buyers post their $0</span></li>
    <li class="row"><span>Colors the buyers mock</span><span>purple, beige</span></li>
    <li class="row"><span>Your buyers</span><span>your page</span></li>
  </ul>
  <p class="row total"><span>Look</span><span class="red">from the research</span></p>
  <p>Every fix for AI slop turns into a look of its own once everyone installs it. lfm ships no style to copy. Each project decides its look in concept.md from its own research: what the buyers post and mock, what every competing page already does. The compiler then holds the page to those decisions. This page chose a receipt because builders keep posting their zeros; <a href="{{.urls.concept}}">its concept</a> shows every choice and the posts behind it.</p>
  <h3>What lfm does not do</h3>
  <p>It brings no traffic and hosts nothing. The research shows where your buyers talk; the page gives the visitors you send a clear way to pay. If every visitor already knows you, a plain page sells too, and lfm keeps that plain page fast, findable and correct.</p>
  <p class="tear" aria-hidden="true"></p>
</article>

<article class="tape" aria-labelledby="agents-h" id="agents">
  <p class="tear tear-in" aria-hidden="true"></p>
  <h2 id="agents-h">If you are an agent</h2>
  <ol class="steps">
    <li>Read <a href="assets/agents/SKILL.md">SKILL.md</a> and follow it.</li>
    <li>Install: <pre><code>{{.data.install.script}}</code></pre></li>
    <li>Run <code>lfm init &lt;offer-name&gt;</code>, then <code>lfm skills install</code> inside it.</li>
    <li>Run <code>lfm compile</code> after every change and do what its last line says until it says deploy.</li>
  </ol>
  <p><code>lfm compile --json</code> returns the same result as JSON, with <code>next.phase</code> and <code>next.skill</code>. Exit 0 means output/ was written. Also here: <a href="assets/agents/llms.txt">llms.txt</a>.</p>
  <p>On Grok Bot, add the <a href="{{.urls.grokBot}}">lfm.cash bot</a>: it installs lfm on its own computer and runs these steps, asking you only what you decide.</p>
  <p class="tear" aria-hidden="true"></p>
</article>

<article class="tape" aria-labelledby="install-h" id="install">
  <p class="tear tear-in" aria-hidden="true"></p>
  <h2 id="install-h">Install it yourself</h2>
  <pre><code>{{.data.install.script}}</code></pre>
  <p>macOS and Linux, Intel and ARM. <a href="assets/agents/install.sh">The script</a> checks the download against the release checksums and puts one binary in ~/.local/bin, without sudo. With Go 1.26 or newer:</p>
  <pre><code>{{.data.install.go}}</code></pre>
  <p>Then, in a new folder:</p>
  <pre><code>lfm init my-page &amp;&amp; cd my-page
lfm skills install
lfm compile</code></pre>
  <p>The skills land in .agents/skills for Codex, Cursor, OpenCode and GitHub Copilot, or .claude/skills for Claude Code. Chrome is optional for <code>lfm compile --release</code>, which scrolls the page at phone and desktop sizes and saves every frame. Docs: <a href="{{.urls.authoring}}">authoring a project</a>, source on <a href="{{.urls.repository}}">GitHub</a>.</p>
  <p class="tear" aria-hidden="true"></p>
</article>

<article class="tape" aria-labelledby="this-h">
  <p class="tear tear-in" aria-hidden="true"></p>
  <h2 id="this-h">This page's receipt</h2>
  <ul class="items">
    <li class="row"><span>Buyer posts</span><span>{{.data.receipt.voices}} from {{.data.receipt.platforms}} sites</span></li>
    <li class="row"><span>Competing pages</span><span>{{.data.receipt.peers}}</span></li>
    <li class="row"><span>Directions</span><span>{{.data.receipt.directions}}, 1 kept</span></li>
    <li class="row"><span>Checks</span><span>{{.data.rules}}, 0 warnings</span></li>
    <li class="row"><span>Frames looked at</span><span>{{.data.receipt.frames}}</span></li>
    <li class="row"><span>HTML</span><span>{{.data.receipt.html}} gzip</span></li>
    <li class="row"><span>Fonts and images</span><span>{{.data.receipt.assets}}</span></li>
    <li class="row"><span>Lighthouse</span><span>{{.data.receipt.lighthouse}}</span></li>
  </ul>
  <p class="row total"><span>Sales today</span><span class="red">$0.00</span></p>
  <p>lfm is free, so this total stays at zero. Yours does not have to. The <a href="{{.urls.voices}}">buyer posts</a>, the <a href="{{.urls.competitors}}">competitor pages</a> and the <a href="{{.urls.concept}}">concept</a> behind this page are in the repository.</p>
  <footer class="foot">
    <p class="rule" aria-hidden="true"></p>
    <p class="row small"><span>LFM: looking for more</span><span><a href="{{.urls.license}}">MIT license</a></span></p>
    <p class="thanks">Thank you. Come back with a sale.</p>
  </footer>
  <p class="tear" aria-hidden="true"></p>
</article>

</main>
{{script "assets/scripts/main.js"}}
</body>
</html>
